.TH BIND 2
.SH NAME
bind, mount, unmount \- change name space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "bind :: proc(source: string, target: string, order: u64) -> i64"
.B "mount :: proc(source: string, target: string, order: u64) -> i64"
.B "unmount :: proc(source: string, target: string) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bind
and
.I mount
change the calling process's own name space, and nobody else's.
After a successful call, evaluating
.I target
continues instead at
.IR source .
.PP
.I Bind
takes both
.I source
and
.I target
from the caller's current name space.
.I Mount
takes
.I source
from
.BR /srv ,
the posted-service directory (see
.IR srv (3)):
.I source
must be a name mount can resolve to a posted service, and after the call
the file tree that service answers for is visible with its root at
.IR target .
.PP
.I Order
says how the new binding joins what is already at
.IR target .
.B abi.ORDER_REPLACE
(0) replaces
.I target
with a union of one member, the new one.
.B ORDER_BEFORE
(1) and
.B ORDER_AFTER
(2) add the new member to the front or the back of the union at
.IR target ,
if
.I target
is already a union directory; a name is looked up in a union's members in
order until it is found.
Any other value is taken as
.BR ORDER_REPLACE .
.PP
A descriptor already open on a file is unaffected by a later bind or
mount: it names a channel to the file, and a bind changes what a
.I path
resolves to.
.PP
.I Unmount
undoes a bind or mount previously made at
.IR target .
An empty
.I source
undoes everything bound or mounted there; a non-empty one undoes only the
binding of that particular source.
.PP
Unlike Plan 9, there are no
.B MCREATE
or
.B MCACHE
flags: a create into a union directory always tries each member in walk
order until one accepts it, and there is no read cache to enable per
mount.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (1),
.IR bind (1),
.IR intro (2),
.IR srv (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Answers zero on success and a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
